-- Basics of Web Designing | Mid 1 | hard | TestId 440009353102019 | theory
INSERT INTO public."TestsData" ("TestId", "Questions", "Options", "Answers")
VALUES (440009353102019, ARRAY['"[8 marks] Analyze a realistic problem involving how the internet and world wide web relate, such as a browser requesting a webpage from a remote server. Present a reasoned approach, justify key decisions, and state an important limitation or verification check."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] Analyze a realistic problem involving website, webpage, web server, domain name and ip address, such as opening example.com by typing its domain in a browser. Present a reasoned approach, justify key decisions, and state an important limitation or verification check."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] Analyze a realistic problem involving purpose of http, https, ftp and tcp/ip, such as choosing a protocol for a login page or transferring a file. Present a reasoned approach, justify key decisions, and state an important limitation or verification check."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] Analyze a realistic problem involving structure of an html5 document, such as creating a valid page with a title and visible paragraph. Present a reasoned approach, justify key decisions, and state an important limitation or verification check."'::jsonb]::jsonb[], ARRAY[]::jsonb[], ARRAY['"Model answer:\nThe Internet is the global network of interconnected networks that communicate using Internet protocols, especially the TCP/IP suite. The World Wide Web is one service that operates over the Internet. Web resources are identified with URLs and commonly transferred using HTTP or HTTPS. A browser sends a request, a web server processes it and returns a response. Other Internet services, such as email and file transfer, are not the same as the Web. A clear answer should distinguish the underlying network from the web pages and linked resources delivered through it.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should explain the governing concept, justify the chosen method, check the result and acknowledge a relevant limitation."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nA webpage is one web document; a website is a collection of related pages and resources. A web server is software or a computer that responds to web requests. A domain name is a human-readable name, while an IP address identifies a network endpoint. DNS resolves a domain name to relevant IP address information. The browser then contacts the server using the appropriate protocol and requests the resource. The concepts are related but are not interchangeable: a domain is not the webpage, and a server is not the IP address.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should explain the governing concept, justify the chosen method, check the result and acknowledge a relevant limitation."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nHTTP transfers web requests and responses; HTTPS uses HTTP over TLS to protect confidentiality and authenticate the server when correctly configured. FTP is designed for file transfer, though secure alternatives are preferred on untrusted networks. TCP provides reliable, ordered delivery, while UDP is connectionless and does not itself guarantee delivery. IP addresses and routes support packet delivery between networks. The protocol should be selected for the task and its security requirements; HTTPS does not repair insecure application logic, and UDP is not a substitute for reliable transport where loss is unacceptable.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should explain the governing concept, justify the chosen method, check the result and acknowledge a relevant limitation."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nA basic HTML5 document starts with `<!DOCTYPE html>`, followed by the `html` root element. The `head` contains metadata such as the character set and the document `title`; the `body` contains the content displayed in the page. Elements use tags and may have attributes. Correct nesting and closing of non-void elements make the document easier to parse and maintain. A suitable page should declare its document type, provide a meaningful title, include required metadata, and place visible content inside the body.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should explain the governing concept, justify the chosen method, check the result and acknowledge a relevant limitation."'::jsonb]::jsonb[]);

-- Basics of Web Designing | Mid 1 | hard | TestId 4400093531020110 | theory
INSERT INTO public."TestsData" ("TestId", "Questions", "Options", "Answers")
VALUES (4400093531020110, ARRAY['"[8 marks] Analyze a realistic problem involving how the internet and world wide web relate, such as a browser requesting a webpage from a remote server. Present a reasoned approach, justify key decisions, and state an important limitation or verification check."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] Analyze a realistic problem involving website, webpage, web server, domain name and ip address, such as opening example.com by typing its domain in a browser. Present a reasoned approach, justify key decisions, and state an important limitation or verification check."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] Analyze a realistic problem involving html elements, attributes and block versus inline content, such as marking a paragraph and emphasizing one word within it. Present a reasoned approach, justify key decisions, and state an important limitation or verification check."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] Analyze a realistic problem involving html headings, paragraphs, images, lists and metadata, such as building a small informational page with an image and steps. Present a reasoned approach, justify key decisions, and state an important limitation or verification check."'::jsonb]::jsonb[], ARRAY[]::jsonb[], ARRAY['"Model answer:\nThe Internet is the global network of interconnected networks that communicate using Internet protocols, especially the TCP/IP suite. The World Wide Web is one service that operates over the Internet. Web resources are identified with URLs and commonly transferred using HTTP or HTTPS. A browser sends a request, a web server processes it and returns a response. Other Internet services, such as email and file transfer, are not the same as the Web. A clear answer should distinguish the underlying network from the web pages and linked resources delivered through it.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should explain the governing concept, justify the chosen method, check the result and acknowledge a relevant limitation."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nA webpage is one web document; a website is a collection of related pages and resources. A web server is software or a computer that responds to web requests. A domain name is a human-readable name, while an IP address identifies a network endpoint. DNS resolves a domain name to relevant IP address information. The browser then contacts the server using the appropriate protocol and requests the resource. The concepts are related but are not interchangeable: a domain is not the webpage, and a server is not the IP address.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should explain the governing concept, justify the chosen method, check the result and acknowledge a relevant limitation."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nAn HTML element is formed from a tag and its content where applicable. Attributes provide additional information in the start tag, such as an image source or a link destination. Block elements commonly start on a new line and occupy the available width, while inline elements flow within surrounding text. For example, `p` is a block element and `span` is inline. Elements should be nested correctly and attributes should be used for their intended purpose. HTML provides structure; styling and presentation are typically handled separately with CSS.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should explain the governing concept, justify the chosen method, check the result and acknowledge a relevant limitation."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nHeadings (`h1`–`h6`) establish a hierarchy; paragraphs use `p`; lists use ordered (`ol`) or unordered (`ul`) containers with `li` items. Images are inserted with `img`; meaningful alternative text in `alt` helps users when the image cannot be seen and supports accessibility. Metadata such as character encoding belongs in `head`, while preformatted text can use `pre` when whitespace matters. Choose elements by meaning rather than visual appearance alone, keep heading levels logical, and provide useful text alternatives for informative images.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should explain the governing concept, justify the chosen method, check the result and acknowledge a relevant limitation."'::jsonb]::jsonb[]);

