nclusion or check. A strong answer should connect the concept to the stated example, explain the steps in order and justify the outcome."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nTextile technology includes fibre preparation, spinning, weaving and finishing. Dyeing adds colour using a dye source and a process suited to the fibre; preparation, temperature, time and mordants can affect shade and colour fastness. Historical Indian textile traditions included sophisticated regional practices, but materials and techniques differed. To analyze a process, identify the fibre, colour source, preparation, application and finishing steps, then explain how these steps influence the result. Avoid treating all traditional dyes or textile methods as chemically or historically identical.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should connect the concept to the stated example, explain the steps in order and justify the outcome."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nTransport systems affect the cost, speed and scale of trade. A historical account may consider land routes, waterways, carts, pack animals, ports, roads, storage, weighing practices, markets and exchange networks. To analyze a trading technique, explain the geography and goods involved, identify how transport and exchange were organized, and consider the effect of distance, season, safety and infrastructure. Evidence such as written records, artifacts and settlement patterns supports claims. Avoid assuming that a method was used uniformly across regions and centuries.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should connect the concept to the stated example, explain the steps in order and justify the outcome."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nTraditional architectural knowledge may discuss site selection, orientation, proportion, layout, construction and the arrangement of spaces in buildings or settlements. Temple architecture has its own forms and symbolic conventions and should not be reduced to a single universal design. The syllabus also covers Ashramas (stages of life), Purusharthas (Dharma, Artha, Kama and Moksha), and ideas of Karma, rebirth and striving for Moksha. A balanced response defines the concepts, places them in their textual or cultural context, and avoids presenting diverse traditions as one unchanging rule set.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should connect the concept to the stated example, explain the steps in order and justify the outcome."'::jsonb]::jsonb[]);

