VALUES (4400991101010135, ARRAY['"[8 marks] Analyze a realistic problem involving metallurgy and iron/steel technology in historical india, such as evaluating an artifact or historical description of iron production. Present a reasoned approach, justify key decisions, and state an important limitation or verification check."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] Analyze a realistic problem involving water management and rainwater harvesting, such as planning a system for collecting seasonal rainfall in a settlement. Present a reasoned approach, justify key decisions, and state an important limitation or verification check."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] Analyze a realistic problem involving historical transport and trading techniques, such as explaining how trade routes influence the movement of goods. Present a reasoned approach, justify key decisions, and state an important limitation or verification check."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] Analyze a realistic problem involving vastu, town planning, architecture and concepts of life, such as comparing the layout of a settlement with the functional design of a building. Present a reasoned approach, justify key decisions, and state an important limitation or verification check."'::jsonb]::jsonb[], ARRAY[]::jsonb[], ARRAY['"Model answer:\nMetallurgy includes obtaining metals from ores and shaping or treating them for use. A historical discussion can cover ore preparation, heating and reduction, forging, joining, heat treatment and the properties sought, while recognizing that techniques varied across periods and regions. Iron and steel production requires evidence about the artifact, process and material; the existence of a notable artifact does not prove that one technique was universal. A well-supported answer connects process to properties such as strength, hardness, corrosion behaviour or workability and identifies the limits of the available evidence.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should explain the governing concept, justify the chosen method, check the result and acknowledge a relevant limitation."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nWater management combines collection, storage, distribution, drainage and maintenance. Rainwater harvesting captures runoff from a roof or catchment, conveys it through channels, filters or settles sediment, and stores or recharges it where appropriate. Traditional systems were adapted to local rainfall, terrain, materials and community needs; Gujarat and other regions contain different examples. A useful analysis checks catchment area, water demand, overflow, contamination prevention and maintenance responsibility. Historical systems should be explained in their local setting rather than assumed to work unchanged in every modern site.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should explain the governing concept, justify the chosen method, check the result and acknowledge a relevant limitation."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nTransport systems affect the cost, speed and scale of trade. A historical account may consider land routes, waterways, carts, pack animals, ports, roads, storage, weighing practices, markets and exchange networks. To analyze a trading technique, explain the geography and goods involved, identify how transport and exchange were organized, and consider the effect of distance, season, safety and infrastructure. Evidence such as written records, artifacts and settlement patterns supports claims. Avoid assuming that a method was used uniformly across regions and centuries.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should explain the governing concept, justify the chosen method, check the result and acknowledge a relevant limitation."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nTraditional architectural knowledge may discuss site selection, orientation, proportion, layout, construction and the arrangement of spaces in buildings or settlements. Temple architecture has its own forms and symbolic conventions and should not be reduced to a single universal design. The syllabus also covers Ashramas (stages of life), Purusharthas (Dharma, Artha, Kama and Moksha), and ideas of Karma, rebirth and striving for Moksha. A balanced response defines the concepts, places them in their textual or cultural context, and avoids presenting diverse traditions as one unchanging rule set.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should explain the governing concept, justify the chosen method, check the result and acknowledge a relevant limitation."'::jsonb]::jsonb[]);

-- Bharatiya Knowledge System | ESE | hard | TestId 4400991101010136 | theory
INSERT INTO public."TestsData" ("TestId", "Questions", "Options", "Answers")
VALUES (4400991101010136, ARRAY['"[8 marks] Analyze a realistic problem involving traditional textile technology and dyeing, such as describing how a natural dye process moves from source material to finished cloth. Present a reasoned approach, justify key decisions, and state an important limitation or verification check."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] Analyze a realistic problem involving water management and rainwater harvesting, such as planning a system for collecting seasonal rainfall in a settlement. Present a reasoned approach, justify key decisions, and state an important limitation or verification check."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] Analyze a realistic problem involving sacred ecology, forests and agricultural knowledge, such as assessing a claim connecting seasonal observations with farm decisions. Present a reasoned approach, justify key decisions, and state an important limitation or verification check."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] Analyze a realistic problem involving vastu, town planning, architecture and concepts of life, such as comparing the layout of a settlement with the functional design of a building. Present a reasoned approach, justify key decisions, and state an important limitation or verification check."'::jsonb]::jsonb[], ARRAY[]::jsonb[], ARRAY['"Model answer:\nTextile technology includes fibre preparation, spinning, weaving and finishing. Dyeing adds colour using a dye source and a process suited to the fibre; preparation, temperature, time and mordants can affect shade and colour fastness. Historical Indian textile traditions included sophisticated regional practices, but materials and techniques differed. To analyze a process, identify the fibre, colour source, preparation, application and finishing steps, then explain how these steps influence the result. Avoid treating all traditional dyes or textile methods as chemically or historically identical.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should explain the governing concept, justify the chosen method, check the result and acknowledge a relevant limitation."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nWater management combines collection, storage, distribution, drainage and maintenance. Rainwater harvesting captures runoff from a roof or catchment, conveys it through channels, filters or settles sediment, and stores or recharges it where appropriate. Traditional systems were adapted to local rainfall, terrain, materials and community needs; Gujarat and other regions contain different examples. A useful analysis checks catchment area, water demand, overflow, contamination prevention and maintenance responsibility. Historical systems should be explained in their local setting rather than assumed to work unchanged in every modern site.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should explain the governing concept, justify the chosen method, check the result and acknowledge a relevant limitation."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nTraditional ecological knowledge may connect land, water, forests, seasonal cycles and social or religious practices. A study of Nakshatra-related agricultural knowledge should identify the particular calendar or observational claim and compare it with the local context and available evidence. Forest management and sacred natural sites can also be studied for their cultural meaning and practical conservation effects. Do not assume that every community followed one identical practice or that cultural association alone proves a measurable ecological outcome. Distinguish historical description, current practice and independently tested environmental effects.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should explain the governing concept, justify the chosen method, check the result and acknowledge a relevant limitation."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nTraditional architectural knowledge may discuss site selection, orientation, proportion, layout, construction and the arrangement of spaces in buildings or settlements. Temple architecture has its own forms and symbolic conventions and should not be reduced to a single universal design. The syllabus also covers Ashramas (stages of life), Purusharthas (Dharma, Artha, Kama and Moksha), and ideas of Karma, rebirth and striving for Moksha. A balanced response defines the concepts, places them in their textual or cultural context, and avoids presenting diverse traditions as one unchanging rule set.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should explain the governing concept, justify the chosen method, check the result and acknowledge a relevant limitation."'::jsonb]::jsonb[]);

-- Communication Skills-I | Mid 1 | easy | TestId 440919120103011 | theory
INSERT INTO public."TestsData" ("TestId", "Questions", "Options", "Answers")
VALUES (440919120103011, ARRAY['"[8 marks] Explain Skimming, scanning, intensive and extensive reading. Describe its meaning or purpose, principal features or steps, and one relevant example."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] Explain Reading comprehension and evidence-based answers. Describe its meaning or purpose, principal features or steps, and one relevant example."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] Explain English tenses and consistency of time. Describe its meaning or purpose, principal features or steps, and one relevant example."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] Explain Parts of speech and their functions. Describe its meaning or purpose, principal features or steps, and one relevant example."'::jsonb]::jsonb[], ARRAY[]::jsonb[], ARRAY['"Model answer:\nSkimming is a rapid read to identify the general idea and structure; scanning searches for a specific detail such as a date or name. Intensive reading examines a short passage carefully for meaning, vocabulary and relationships, while extensive reading builds fluency through longer texts for general understanding. Select the technique to match the purpose, preview headings or key words, and return to the passage for evidence before answering. These methods complement rather than replace one another; a reader often skims first, scans for details and then reads a critical section intensively.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should define the concept and organize the main points clearly."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nComprehension requires identifying the central idea, supporting details, sequence, purpose, tone and implied meaning. Direct questions can often be answered from an explicit sentence; inference questions require connecting evidence and background understanding without inventing facts. Read the question carefully, locate relevant lines, distinguish fact from opinion and summarize in one''s own words when appropriate. A correct answer should be supported by the passage, not merely by personal belief. For unfamiliar vocabulary, use surrounding sentences and word structure as clues, then verify the interpretation in context.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should define the concept and organize the main points clearly."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nTense and aspect locate an action in time and describe whether it is ongoing, completed or connected to another time. Simple forms express facts or events; continuous forms emphasize duration; perfect forms connect an earlier action with a reference time. Maintain consistent time references unless the meaning requires a shift. For example, a report of a completed visit generally uses past forms for events, while a general conclusion may use present tense. Check time expressions and the intended chronology when choosing a verb form.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should define the concept and organize the main points clearly."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nNouns name people, places, things or ideas; pronouns replace noun phrases; verbs express actions or states; adjectives modify nouns; adverbs modify verbs, adjectives or other adverbs. Prepositions express relationships, conjunctions connect units, and interjections express reactions. A word''s part of speech depends on how it functions in the sentence, not only on its spelling. During editing, identify the word''s role and check whether modifiers are placed clearly and whether each clause is complete.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should define the concept and organize the main points clearly."'::jsonb]::jsonb[]);

-- Communication Skills-I | Mid 1 | easy | TestId 440919120103012 | theory
INSERT INTO public."TestsData" ("TestId", "Questions", "Options", "Answers")
VALUES (440919120103012, ARRAY['"[8 marks] Explain Skimming, scanning, intensive and extensive reading. Describe its meaning or purpose, principal features or steps, and one relevant example."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] Explain Reading comprehension and evidence-based answers. Describe its meaning or purpose, principal features or steps, and one relevant example."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] Explain Subject–verb agreement and modal verbs. Describe its meaning or purpose, principal features or steps, and one relevant example."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] Explain Active and passive voice and sentence types. Describe its meaning or purpose, principal features or steps, and one relevant example."'::jsonb]::jsonb[], ARRAY[]::jsonb[], ARRAY['"Model answer:\nSkimming is a rapid read to identify the general idea and structure; scanning searches for a specific detail such as a date or name. Intensive reading examines a short passage carefully for meaning, vocabulary and relationships, while extensive reading builds fluency through longer texts for general understanding. Select the technique to match the purpose, preview headings or key words, and return to the passage for evidence before answering. These methods complement rather than replace one another; a reader often skims first, scans for details and then reads a critical section intensively.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should define the concept and organize the main points clearly."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nComprehension requires identifying the central idea, supporting details, sequence, purpose, tone and implied meaning. Direct questions can often be answered from an explicit sentence; inference questions require connecting evidence and background understanding without inventing facts. Read the question carefully, locate relevant lines, distinguish fact from opinion and summarize in one''s own words when appropriate. A correct answer should be supported by the passage, not merely by personal belief. For unfamiliar vocabulary, use surrounding sentences and word structure as clues, then verify the interpretation in context.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should define the concept and organize the main points clearly."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nA finite verb should agree with its subject in person and number. The subject may be separated from the verb by a phrase, so identify the head noun rather than matching the nearest word. Indefinite pronouns, compound subjects and collective nouns require attention to the intended meaning and applicable convention. Modals such as can, could, may, might, must, should and would express ability, possibility, permission, obligation or advice and are followed by the base form of the main verb. Editing should preserve the intended meaning.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should define the concept and organize the main points clearly."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nIn active voice, the subject performs the action; in passive voice, the subject receives the action. A passive construction commonly uses a suitable form of `be` plus the past participle, and the agent can be omitted when unknown or unimportant. The tense and meaning must be preserved when converting voice. Sentences may be declarative, interrogative, imperative or exclamatory; clauses and punctuation should match the intended function. Prefer active voice when responsibility is important, and passive voice when the process or result deserves emphasis.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should define the concept and organize the main points clearly."'::jsonb]::jsonb[]);

