-- Communication Skills-I | Mid 1 | medium | TestId 440919120103017 | theory
INSERT INTO public."TestsData" ("TestId", "Questions", "Options", "Answers")
VALUES (440919120103017, ARRAY['"[8 marks] A student must apply skimming, scanning, intensive and extensive reading in a practical situation such as finding a date quickly before reading a technical article in depth. Explain the method or reasoning, set out the main steps, and justify the result."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] A student must apply english tenses and consistency of time in a practical situation such as describing a completed lab visit and a result that is still valid. Explain the method or reasoning, set out the main steps, and justify the result."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] A student must apply subject–verb agreement and modal verbs in a practical situation such as revising a sentence about a group of students and their responsibilities. Explain the method or reasoning, set out the main steps, and justify the result."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] A student must apply active and passive voice and sentence types in a practical situation such as turning a process instruction into a report of what was done. Explain the method or reasoning, set out the main steps, and justify the result."'::jsonb]::jsonb[], ARRAY[]::jsonb[], ARRAY['"Model answer:\nSkimming is a rapid read to identify the general idea and structure; scanning searches for a specific detail such as a date or name. Intensive reading examines a short passage carefully for meaning, vocabulary and relationships, while extensive reading builds fluency through longer texts for general understanding. Select the technique to match the purpose, preview headings or key words, and return to the passage for evidence before answering. These methods complement rather than replace one another; a reader often skims first, scans for details and then reads a critical section intensively.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should connect the concept to the stated example, explain the steps in order and justify the outcome."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nTense and aspect locate an action in time and describe whether it is ongoing, completed or connected to another time. Simple forms express facts or events; continuous forms emphasize duration; perfect forms connect an earlier action with a reference time. Maintain consistent time references unless the meaning requires a shift. For example, a report of a completed visit generally uses past forms for events, while a general conclusion may use present tense. Check time expressions and the intended chronology when choosing a verb form.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should connect the concept to the stated example, explain the steps in order and justify the outcome."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nA finite verb should agree with its subject in person and number. The subject may be separated from the verb by a phrase, so identify the head noun rather than matching the nearest word. Indefinite pronouns, compound subjects and collective nouns require attention to the intended meaning and applicable convention. Modals such as can, could, may, might, must, should and would express ability, possibility, permission, obligation or advice and are followed by the base form of the main verb. Editing should preserve the intended meaning.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should connect the concept to the stated example, explain the steps in order and justify the outcome."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nIn active voice, the subject performs the action; in passive voice, the subject receives the action. A passive construction commonly uses a suitable form of `be` plus the past participle, and the agent can be omitted when unknown or unimportant. The tense and meaning must be preserved when converting voice. Sentences may be declarative, interrogative, imperative or exclamatory; clauses and punctuation should match the intended function. Prefer active voice when responsibility is important, and passive voice when the process or result deserves emphasis.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should connect the concept to the stated example, explain the steps in order and justify the outcome."'::jsonb]::jsonb[]);

-- Communication Skills-I | Mid 1 | medium | TestId 440919120103018 | theory
INSERT INTO public."TestsData" ("TestId", "Questions", "Options", "Answers")
VALUES (440919120103018, ARRAY['"[8 marks] A student must apply reading comprehension and evidence-based answers in a practical situation such as answering an inference question from a passage about a new campus service. Explain the method or reasoning, set out the main steps, and justify the result."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] A student must apply english tenses and consistency of time in a practical situation such as describing a completed lab visit and a result that is still valid. Explain the method or reasoning, set out the main steps, and justify the result."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] A student must apply parts of speech and their functions in a practical situation such as editing a sentence where a modifier is attached to the wrong word. Explain the method or reasoning, set out the main steps, and justify the result."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] A student must apply active and passive voice and sentence types in a practical situation such as turning a process instruction into a report of what was done. Explain the method or reasoning, set out the main steps, and justify the result."'::jsonb]::jsonb[], ARRAY[]::jsonb[], ARRAY['"Model answer:\nComprehension requires identifying the central idea, supporting details, sequence, purpose, tone and implied meaning. Direct questions can often be answered from an explicit sentence; inference questions require connecting evidence and background understanding without inventing facts. Read the question carefully, locate relevant lines, distinguish fact from opinion and summarize in one''s own words when appropriate. A correct answer should be supported by the passage, not merely by personal belief. For unfamiliar vocabulary, use surrounding sentences and word structure as clues, then verify the interpretation in context.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should connect the concept to the stated example, explain the steps in order and justify the outcome."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nTense and aspect locate an action in time and describe whether it is ongoing, completed or connected to another time. Simple forms express facts or events; continuous forms emphasize duration; perfect forms connect an earlier action with a reference time. Maintain consistent time references unless the meaning requires a shift. For example, a report of a completed visit generally uses past forms for events, while a general conclusion may use present tense. Check time expressions and the intended chronology when choosing a verb form.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should connect the concept to the stated example, explain the steps in order and justify the outcome."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nNouns name people, places, things or ideas; pronouns replace noun phrases; verbs express actions or states; adjectives modify nouns; adverbs modify verbs, adjectives or other adverbs. Prepositions express relationships, conjunctions connect units, and interjections express reactions. A word''s part of speech depends on how it functions in the sentence, not only on its spelling. During editing, identify the word''s role and check whether modifiers are placed clearly and whether each clause is complete.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should connect the concept to the stated example, explain the steps in order and justify the outcome."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nIn active voice, the subject performs the action; in passive voice, the subject receives the action. A passive construction commonly uses a suitable form of `be` plus the past participle, and the agent can be omitted when unknown or unimportant. The tense and meaning must be preserved when converting voice. Sentences may be declarative, interrogative, imperative or exclamatory; clauses and punctuation should match the intended function. Prefer active voice when responsibility is important, and passive voice when the process or result deserves emphasis.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should connect the concept to the stated example, explain the steps in order and justify the outcome."'::jsonb]::jsonb[]);

