-- Engineering Drawing | Mid 1 | medium | TestId 440091351101016 | theory
INSERT INTO public."TestsData" ("TestId", "Questions", "Options", "Answers")
VALUES (440091351101016, ARRAY['"[8 marks] A student must apply engineering curves and their practical classification in a practical situation such as selecting a curve construction for a cam profile or motion diagram. Explain the method or reasoning, set out the main steps, and justify the result."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] A student must apply constructing an ellipse by standard geometric methods in a practical situation such as drawing an ellipse when the major and minor axes are known. Explain the method or reasoning, set out the main steps, and justify the result."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] A student must apply projection of points in the principal planes and quadrants in a practical situation such as plotting points located above/below HP and in front of/behind VP. Explain the method or reasoning, set out the main steps, and justify the result."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] A student must apply projection of lines and finding true length and inclination in a practical situation such as projecting a line inclined to both HP and VP. Explain the method or reasoning, set out the main steps, and justify the result."'::jsonb]::jsonb[], ARRAY[]::jsonb[], ARRAY['"Model answer:\nEngineering curves include conics and other geometric curves used to represent shapes or motion. Ellipses, parabolas and hyperbolas are conic sections; cycloids, epicycloids, hypocycloids, involutes and spirals are other common constructions in engineering graphics. The method is chosen according to the defining geometry and the intended use. A drawing should show construction lines clearly, use suitable instruments and identify the required tangent or normal when requested. Accuracy depends on the construction procedure and neat, unambiguous line work.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should connect the concept to the stated example, explain the steps in order and justify the outcome."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nAn ellipse can be constructed by methods such as concentric circles, rectangle or oblong methods, and arc-of-circle approaches taught in the syllabus. First mark the major and minor axes and their centres accurately. Divide the relevant circles, sides or arcs into corresponding parts, project matching points and draw a smooth continuous curve through them. Select the method according to the data and construction requested. Check symmetry about both axes and use light construction lines before darkening the final curve.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should connect the concept to the stated example, explain the steps in order and justify the outcome."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nOrthographic projection represents a 3D point by projections on principal reference planes, commonly the horizontal plane (HP) and vertical plane (VP). A point''s position relative to HP and VP determines its quadrant and the location of its front and top views. Use the reference line consistently, draw projectors perpendicular to it and apply the convention for distances from each plane. Label views clearly and preserve the given dimensions. Errors often result from swapping the meanings of distance above/below HP and in front of/behind VP.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should connect the concept to the stated example, explain the steps in order and justify the outcome."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nA line parallel to a reference plane appears in true length on that plane''s projection; when inclined, its projection is generally foreshortened. For a line inclined to one or both planes, use the prescribed change-of-position or component method, keeping endpoints and projectors consistent. True length and true inclinations are recovered from a view in which the line''s full length is represented or from the appropriate construction. Show intermediate positions and label views. Check that apparent projected length does not exceed true length and that given endpoint coordinates remain consistent.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should connect the concept to the stated example, explain the steps in order and justify the outcome."'::jsonb]::jsonb[]);

