INSERT INTO public."TestsData" ("TestId", "Questions", "Options", "Answers")
VALUES (440091351101018, ARRAY['"[8 marks] A student must apply constructing an ellipse by standard geometric methods in a practical situation such as drawing an ellipse when the major and minor axes are known. Explain the method or reasoning, set out the main steps, and justify the result."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] A student must apply parabola and hyperbola construction and the focus/directrix idea in a practical situation such as constructing a conic using a given focus and directrix. Explain the method or reasoning, set out the main steps, and justify the result."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] A student must apply cycloidal curves, involutes and spirals in a practical situation such as representing a point on a rolling circle or an unwinding string. Explain the method or reasoning, set out the main steps, and justify the result."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] A student must apply projection of lines and finding true length and inclination in a practical situation such as projecting a line inclined to both HP and VP. Explain the method or reasoning, set out the main steps, and justify the result."'::jsonb]::jsonb[], ARRAY[]::jsonb[], ARRAY['"Model answer:\nAn ellipse can be constructed by methods such as concentric circles, rectangle or oblong methods, and arc-of-circle approaches taught in the syllabus. First mark the major and minor axes and their centres accurately. Divide the relevant circles, sides or arcs into corresponding parts, project matching points and draw a smooth continuous curve through them. Select the method according to the data and construction requested. Check symmetry about both axes and use light construction lines before darkening the final curve.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should connect the concept to the stated example, explain the steps in order and justify the outcome."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nA conic is defined by a geometric relation between a point and a focus/directrix or by another specified construction condition. A parabola has eccentricity one; an ellipse has eccentricity below one and a hyperbola above one. Construction methods use the given dimensions or geometric properties to locate successive points, then join them smoothly. Tangents and normals are drawn according to the prescribed method at the required point. Verify the defining dimensions and symmetry and distinguish the conics by their geometry rather than by appearance alone.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should connect the concept to the stated example, explain the steps in order and justify the outcome."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nA cycloid is traced by a point on a circle rolling along a straight line. An epicycloid is generated when a circle rolls outside another circle, while a hypocycloid is generated when it rolls inside. An involute can be understood as the path traced by the end of a taut string unwound from a base curve; spirals move progressively inward or outward according to their construction. Start from the generating geometry, divide the motion into corresponding steps, plot points carefully and construct any requested tangent or normal.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should connect the concept to the stated example, explain the steps in order and justify the outcome."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nA line parallel to a reference plane appears in true length on that plane''s projection; when inclined, its projection is generally foreshortened. For a line inclined to one or both planes, use the prescribed change-of-position or component method, keeping endpoints and projectors consistent. True length and true inclinations are recovered from a view in which the line''s full length is represented or from the appropriate construction. Show intermediate positions and label views. Check that apparent projected length does not exceed true length and that given endpoint coordinates remain consistent.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should connect the concept to the stated example, explain the steps in order and justify the outcome."'::jsonb]::jsonb[]);

