-- Engineering Drawing | ESE | easy | TestId 4400913511010125 | theory
INSERT INTO public."TestsData" ("TestId", "Questions", "Options", "Answers")
VALUES (4400913511010125, ARRAY['"[8 marks] Explain Drawing instruments, BIS conventions, line types and layout. Describe its meaning or purpose, principal features or steps, and one relevant example."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] Explain Representative fraction and selection of drawing scales. Describe its meaning or purpose, principal features or steps, and one relevant example."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] Explain Dimensioning and annotation of engineering drawings. Describe its meaning or purpose, principal features or steps, and one relevant example."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] Explain Orthographic projection and first-angle versus third-angle methods. Describe its meaning or purpose, principal features or steps, and one relevant example."'::jsonb]::jsonb[], ARRAY[]::jsonb[], ARRAY['"Model answer:\nA drawing sheet should have a suitable size, border, title block and layout, with space allocated for views and notes. Instruments such as drawing boards, T-squares or parallel rules, set squares, compass, divider and scale support accurate construction. Standard line conventions distinguish visible outlines, hidden edges, centre lines, dimensions and construction work. Use lettering and dimensioning consistently and choose a suitable scale when the object will not fit at full size. Standards make drawings legible and interpretable by others, rather than relying on informal assumptions.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should define the concept and organize the main points clearly."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nRepresentative fraction (RF) is drawing length divided by actual length after both are expressed in the same units. A full-size scale has RF 1:1; a reducing scale has RF below one, and an enlarging scale has RF greater than one. For example, RF 1:5 means a 50 mm actual length is shown as 10 mm on the drawing. Select a scale that preserves legibility and available sheet space, state it clearly, and apply it consistently to dimensions and construction. The written dimension normally states actual size, not the measured paper length.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should define the concept and organize the main points clearly."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nDimensioning communicates the size and position of features. Use clear extension and dimension lines, legible figures and appropriate symbols, and avoid repeating dimensions or creating ambiguity. Choose line types according to their purpose: visible outlines, hidden details, centre lines and dimension lines should be distinguishable. Annotations should be placed near the relevant feature without clutter. A drawing should contain enough dimensions to manufacture or inspect the part, but not contradictory or redundant values. Apply the relevant standard conventions and check units and overall dimensions.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should define the concept and organize the main points clearly."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nOrthographic projection represents an object through views perpendicular to principal planes. The front view communicates height and width, the top view width and depth, and a side view height and depth. In first-angle projection, the object is between the observer and projection plane; in third-angle projection, the projection plane is between observer and object. Therefore the arrangement of views differs. Identify the projection method by its symbol or stated convention, align corresponding dimensions across views, and check that the set of views describes one consistent object.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should define the concept and organize the main points clearly."'::jsonb]::jsonb[]);

-- Engineering Drawing | ESE | easy | TestId 4400913511010126 | theory
INSERT INTO public."TestsData" ("TestId", "Questions", "Options", "Answers")
VALUES (4400913511010126, ARRAY['"[8 marks] Explain Drawing instruments, BIS conventions, line types and layout. Describe its meaning or purpose, principal features or steps, and one relevant example."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] Explain Representative fraction and selection of drawing scales. Describe its meaning or purpose, principal features or steps, and one relevant example."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] Explain Sectional views and interpretation of internal features. Describe its meaning or purpose, principal features or steps, and one relevant example."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] Explain Isometric scale, isometric projection and isometric view. Describe its meaning or purpose, principal features or steps, and one relevant example."'::jsonb]::jsonb[], ARRAY[]::jsonb[], ARRAY['"Model answer:\nA drawing sheet should have a suitable size, border, title block and layout, with space allocated for views and notes. Instruments such as drawing boards, T-squares or parallel rules, set squares, compass, divider and scale support accurate construction. Standard line conventions distinguish visible outlines, hidden edges, centre lines, dimensions and construction work. Use lettering and dimensioning consistently and choose a suitable scale when the object will not fit at full size. Standards make drawings legible and interpretable by others, rather than relying on informal assumptions.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should define the concept and organize the main points clearly."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nRepresentative fraction (RF) is drawing length divided by actual length after both are expressed in the same units. A full-size scale has RF 1:1; a reducing scale has RF below one, and an enlarging scale has RF greater than one. For example, RF 1:5 means a 50 mm actual length is shown as 10 mm on the drawing. Select a scale that preserves legibility and available sheet space, state it clearly, and apply it consistently to dimensions and construction. The written dimension normally states actual size, not the measured paper length.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should define the concept and organize the main points clearly."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nA sectional view imagines the object cut by a defined plane and removes the nearer part to reveal internal geometry. The cutting plane and viewing direction must be clear; hatching indicates surfaces intersected by the cutting plane and should be spaced consistently. A section can reduce the number of hidden lines and make holes, cavities or internal profiles easier to interpret. Do not hatch empty spaces, and follow the applicable convention for adjacent parts. Compare the section with other views to ensure that the revealed internal form is geometrically consistent.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should define the concept and organize the main points clearly."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nAn isometric drawing shows three principal axes equally inclined in the representation, commonly 120 degrees apart. Isometric projection uses foreshortened lengths along the axes and therefore an isometric scale; an isometric view/drawing often uses true lengths along isometric axes as prescribed in the course. To construct an object, establish the axes, transfer width, depth and height from the orthographic views, add faces and circular features with suitable isometric constructions, then remove unnecessary construction lines. Check that all dimensions correspond to the original orthographic views.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should define the concept and organize the main points clearly."'::jsonb]::jsonb[]);

