ature enacts a law, and the executive implements it. A student claims the Election Commission is a third lawmaking House. Which correction is correct?"'::jsonb, '"A parent fails to provide educational opportunities to a child aged 8. Which pair most accurately separates a Fundamental Duty from the related right?"'::jsonb, '"A new state policy is consistent with a Directive Principle but is implemented in a way that violates Article 21. What follows?"'::jsonb, '"A person says all Directive Principles are individually enforceable in court because they are ''fundamental in governance''. Which reasoning corrects the conflation?"'::jsonb, '"A city municipal election and a Lok Sabha general election are both due. Which allocation matches the Constitution''s institutional design?"'::jsonb, '"A law classifies citizens by a criterion unrelated to its stated goal; affected people also claim the criterion burdens an Article 19 freedom. What is the strongest analytical approach?"'::jsonb, '"A Fundamental Duty to protect the environment is cited to automatically deny a person''s otherwise lawful speech. Which conclusion is most careful?"'::jsonb, '"A law limits a citizen''s expression, uses a classification that may be arbitrary, and is defended as advancing a public-policy objective. Which review sequence is most defensible?"'::jsonb, '"A government scheme promotes social welfare, but its implementation treats similarly situated citizens arbitrarily. Which constitutional analysis is strongest?"'::jsonb, '"An administrator relies on an environmental duty to justify all restrictions on a public meeting. What should a balanced legal analysis do?"'::jsonb, '"A claimant argues that because a policy appears in Part IV, courts must enforce it as if it were a Fundamental Right. Which constitutional text most directly challenges that claim?"'::jsonb, '"A city and a rural district are designing local institutions. Which pairing is most consistent with the syllabus?"'::jsonb, '"A student 