-- Indian Constitution | Mid 2 | hard | TestId 4400912101010124 | mcq
INSERT INTO public."TestsData" ("TestId", "Questions", "Options", "Answers")
VALUES (4400912101010124, ARRAY['"An interpretation dispute involves an ambiguous provision. One interpretation better advances justice, liberty and equality while remaining consistent with the text. How may the Preamble be used?"'::jsonb, '"A citizen challenges an order affecting personal liberty. The authority claims Article 21 only applies when a law is completely absent, not when procedure exists but is arbitrary. Which response best reflects modern constitutional reasoning at course level?"'::jsonb, '"A student states the Election Commission of India administers municipal elections because Article 324 concerns election control. Which constitutional arrangement resolves the point?"'::jsonb, '"A state Governor and a Chief Minister disagree about the ordinary political direction of a state government. Which principle best describes their normal roles?"'::jsonb, '"An official says Article 14 guarantees identical treatment even where relevant circumstances differ. Which statement is more accurate?"'::jsonb, '"A parent fails to provide educational opportunities to a child aged 8. Which pair most accurately separates a Fundamental Duty from the related right?"'::jsonb, '"A person cites a Directive Principle as the sole basis to demand a directly enforceable order. Which qualification should be included?"'::jsonb, '"The Union government loses Lok Sabha confidence but claims the Council of Ministers answers only to Rajya Sabha. Which constitutional feature refutes the claim?"'::jsonb, '"A city and a rural district are designing local institutions. Which pairing is most consistent with the syllabus?"'::jsonb, '"The Preamble''s words ''Secular'' and ''Socialist'' were inserted by an amendment. Which statement about that history is correct?"'::jsonb, '"A law restricts a citizen''s speech to address a public concern. The citizen argues Article 19 makes all restrictions invalid. Which reasoning is most defensible?"'::jsonb, '"A local administration proposal mixes city drainage services and district rural development. Which institutional design best reflects the course distinctions?"'::jsonb, '"A person says all Directive Principles are individually enforceable in court because they are ''fundamental in governance''. Which reasoning corrects the conflation?"'::jsonb, '"A local government officer claims their administrative decisions are outside constitutional limits because the office is local, not national. Which principle best answers that claim?"'::jsonb, '"An administrator relies on an environmental duty to justify all restrictions on a public meeting. What should a balanced legal analysis do?"'::jsonb, '"A new state policy is consistent with a Directive Principle but is implemented in a way that violates Article 21. What follows?"'::jsonb, '"A student says the ECI is responsible for every municipal poll because Article 324 covers elections. What is the best correction?"'::jsonb, '"An amendment is challenged as undermining a foundational feature, but the challenger merely says the new text is different. What must the argument add?"'::jsonb, '"A law classifies citizens by a criterion unrelated to its stated goal; affected people also claim the criterion burdens an Article 19 freedom. What is the strongest analytical approach?"'::jsonb, '"A state policy seeks environmental improvement, but an official arbitrarily denies access to a public benefit. What is a balanced analysis?"'::jsonb, '"An amendment changes an institution in a way alleged to eliminate judicial review and constitutional limits. Which framework most directly addresses whether a constitutional amendment can destroy foundational features?"'::jsonb, '"A claimant argues that because a policy appears in Part IV, courts must enforce it as if it were a Fundamental Right. Which constitutional text most directly challenges that claim?"'::jsonb, '"A legislature enacts a law, and the executive implements it. A student claims the Election Commission is a third lawmaking House. Which correction is correct?"'::jsonb, '"An amendment is challenged as abolishing the essential democratic character of the Constitution. Which sequence of analysis is most appropriate for a course-level answer?"'::jsonb, '"A person is denied personal liberty under an executive order with no lawful procedure. The authority says that administrative convenience is sufficient. Which response best reflects Article 21?"'::jsonb, '"A constitutional issue concerns equal treatment, personal liberty and a policy directive. Which response is methodologically sound?"'::jsonb, '"The Council of Ministers remains in office despite losing Lok Sabha confidence, and the executive refuses to account to the House. Which principle is most directly implicated?"'::jsonb, '"A welfare scheme relies on a Directive Principle and denies a benefit through an arbitrary process. What is the most accurate conclusion?"'::jsonb, '"A person challenges a deprivation of liberty and an arbitrary classification used to justify it. Which pair of Articles provides the most direct starting points?"'::jsonb, '"An election dispute concerns a Lok Sabha constituency, while a separate dispute concerns a municipal ward poll. Which institutional distinction is relevant at the outset?"'::jsonb]::jsonb[], ARRAY['"As a substitute for all judicial reasoning"'::jsonb, '"As a local administration manual"'::jsonb, '"As an authority to disregard the operative text"'::jsonb, '"As an interpretive aid that illuminates constitutional objectives while the provision''s text remains controlling"'::jsonb, '"Article 21 protection includes scrutiny of the legal procedure affecting life and liberty; the existence of a procedure alone does not end analysis"'::jsonb, '"Article 21 only applies during elections"'::jsonb, '"Article 14 and 21 cannot ever be considered together"'::jsonb, '"Any written procedure automatically validates every deprivation"'::jsonb, '"Part IXA provides a State Election Commission framework for municipality elections, while ECI handles elections assigned under Article 324"'::jsonb, '"Municipal elections are outside the Constitution entirely"'::jsonb, '"Municipal Corporation appoints ECI members"'::jsonb, '"Lok Sabha runs municipal wards directly"'::jsonb, '"The District Collector is the constitutional head of the state"'::jsonb, '"The Chief Minister is appointed by the municipal corporation"'::jsonb, '"The Governor is constitutional head while the Chief Minister leads the elected government and Council of Ministers"'::jsonb, '"The Governor is the elected day-to-day political leader"'::jsonb, '"Equality requires equal protection and bars arbitrary discrimination, but reasonable classification may be constitutionally permitted"'::jsonb, '"Article 14 only concerns local elections"'::jsonb, '"Article 14 abolishes all classification"'::jsonb, '"Article 14 applies only to elected officials"'::jsonb, '"The 73rd Amendment creates both"'::jsonb, '"Article 51A(k) states the parental/guardian duty; Article 21A concerns the right to education for ages 6–14"'::jsonb, '"Article 14 is the duty and Article 19 is the right"'::jsonb, '"Article 324 is the duty and Article 51A is a right"'::jsonb, '"They apply only to local councils"'::jsonb, '"They guide State policy and are important in governance, but Article 37 says they are not directly enforceable by courts"'::jsonb, '"Directive Principles are superior to every constitutional right"'::jsonb, '"They are listed in Article 324"'::jsonb, '"State Governors supervise Union ministers"'::jsonb, '"Collective responsibility to Lok Sabha"'::jsonb, '"The State Election Commission''s municipal remit"'::jsonb, '"The President presides over Rajya Sabha daily"'::jsonb, '"Both bodies are election courts"'::jsonb, '"Municipal Corporation—urban local government; Zila Panchayat—district-level rural local government"'::jsonb, '"Zila Panchayat—House of Parliament; Municipal Corporation—state Cabinet"'::jsonb, '"Municipal Corporation—rural district; Zila Panchayat—Union legislature"'::jsonb, '"They are found only in Article 324"'::jsonb, '"They were added to the original text in 1947"'::jsonb, '"They were included only by the 86th Amendment"'::jsonb, '"They were added by the 42nd Amendment in 1976"'::jsonb, '"Declare every speech restriction invalid without examining the provision"'::jsonb, '"Decide solely by the location of the person''s district"'::jsonb, '"Identify the relevant Article 19 freedom and test whether the restriction is reasonable and falls within a constitutionally permitted ground"'::jsonb, '"Treat the restriction as a Fundamental Duty"'::jsonb, '"Give both tasks to the Rajya Sabha"'::jsonb, '"Assign both to the Election Commission of India"'::jsonb, '"Treat both as functions of the Prime Minister personally"'::jsonb, '"Use the Municipal Corporation for urban local-government functions and the Zila Panchayat for district-level rural local government, subject to applicable state arrangements"'::jsonb, '"Article 37 expressly distinguishes their governance importance from direct court enforceability"'::jsonb, '"Part IV is actually Part III"'::jsonb, '"Only the President can decide enforceability"'::jsonb, '"Article 51A makes every policy court-enforceable"'::jsonb, '"Only Parliament must follow law"'::jsonb, '"Public authorities at all levels must act within applicable constitutional and legal limits"'::jsonb, '"Constitutionalism applies only during general elections"'::jsonb, '"Fundamental Rights apply only to Union ministers"'::jsonb, '"Treat Article 51A as the only enforceable right"'::jsonb, '"Reject environmental protection as a constitutional concern"'::jsonb, '"Assume public meetings are always prohibited"'::jsonb, '"Recognize Article 51A''s environmental duty but independently assess the restriction under the applicable rights and lawful procedure"'::jsonb, '"Its policy objective alone does not eliminate the need to comply with applicable Fundamental Rights"'::jsonb, '"Article 21 does not apply to government action"'::jsonb, '"Part IV automatically overrides Part III"'::jsonb, '"Only Parliament''s Speaker can evaluate it"'::jsonb, '"No constitutional body administers local elections"'::jsonb, '"The Constitution separately provides State Election Commissions for Panchayat and municipal elections"'::jsonb, '"Article 324 covers only school elections"'::jsonb, '"Municipal polls are run by Lok Sabha"'::jsonb, '"The amendment''s page count"'::jsonb, '"Only the date it was introduced"'::jsonb, '"A reasoned connection between the amendment''s practical/legal effect and an alleged basic feature"'::jsonb, '"The names of all members of the legislature"'::jsonb, '"Assess equality/non-arbitrariness under Article 14 and separately test any Article 19 restriction against its applicable constitutional grounds"'::jsonb, '"Assume a single Article automatically decides all claims"'::jsonb, '"Treat the matter only as a Fundamental Duty"'::jsonb, '"Refer the policy to a Zila Panchayat for constitutional interpretation"'::jsonb, '"Treat the environmental aim as an automatic exemption from equality"'::jsonb, '"Assume Article 14 applies only during elections"'::jsonb, '"Recognize the environmental aim and Article 51A duty, while also assessing whether the decision complies with applicable rights such as Article 14"'::jsonb, '"Assume Fundamental Duties erase all legal procedures"'::jsonb, '"The municipal corporation framework"'::jsonb, '"The basic structure doctrine"'::jsonb, '"The ordinary rule for email notices"'::jsonb, '"Article 51A only"'::jsonb, '"Article 324"'::jsonb, '"Article 37"'::jsonb, '"Article 51A(k)"'::jsonb, '"Article 21"'::jsonb, '"The ECI is the Union Council of Ministers"'::jsonb, '"The ECI is the court of final appeal in every dispute"'::jsonb, '"The ECI replaces Rajya Sabha in election years"'::jsonb, '"The ECI is a constitutional election authority, not a House of Parliament"'::jsonb, '"Ask the municipal corporation to ratify the amendment"'::jsonb, '"Check only whether the amendment title contains the word democratic"'::jsonb, '"Identify the alleged basic feature and assess it under the basic structure doctrine"'::jsonb, '"Treat every amendment as automatically invalid"'::jsonb, '"Article 21 concerns only the right to vote"'::jsonb, '"Convenience always overrides liberty"'::jsonb, '"Personal liberty cannot be deprived except according to procedure established by law"'::jsonb, '"Article 21 applies only to corporations"'::jsonb, '"Refer it to a municipal corporation as the final constitutional court"'::jsonb, '"Apply only Article 51A to every dispute"'::jsonb, '"Assume Part IV repeals Part III"'::jsonb, '"Identify the separate questions under Article 14, Article 21 and Part IV, then interpret them together rather than treating one as automatic trump"'::jsonb, '"Collective responsibility of the Union Council of Ministers to Lok Sabha"'::jsonb, '"Municipal administration"'::jsonb, '"The Preamble''s wording about fraternity only"'::jsonb, '"Fundamental Duty to protect the environment"'::jsonb, '"The denial can never be reviewed"'::jsonb, '"Directive Principles are meaningless and must be ignored"'::jsonb, '"The scheme''s objective may be relevant, but its arbitrary implementation can still raise Article 14 concerns"'::jsonb, '"Because welfare is a goal, all procedures are valid"'::jsonb, '"Articles 243K and 243ZA"'::jsonb, '"Articles 73 and 74"'::jsonb, '"Articles 51A and 324"'::jsonb, '"Articles 14 and 21"'::jsonb, '"ECI administers the parliamentary election; State EC administers municipal elections under the local-body framework"'::jsonb, '"Both are conducted only by the Supreme Court"'::jsonb, '"The President organizes both directly"'::jsonb, '"Both are necessarily conducted by Municipal Corporation itself"'::jsonb]::jsonb[], ARRAY['3'::jsonb, '0'::jsonb, '0'::jsonb, '2'::jsonb, '0'::jsonb, '1'::jsonb, '1'::jsonb, '1'::jsonb, '1'::jsonb, '3'::jsonb, '2'::jsonb, '3'::jsonb, '0'::jsonb, '1'::jsonb, '3'::jsonb, '0'::jsonb, '1'::jsonb, '2'::jsonb, '0'::jsonb, '2'::jsonb, '1'::jsonb, '1'::jsonb, '3'::jsonb, '2'::jsonb, '2'::jsonb, '3'::jsonb, '0'::jsonb, '2'::jsonb, '3'::jsonb, '0'::jsonb]::jsonb[]);

-- Mathematics-I | Mid 1 | easy | TestId 440091312101011 | theory
INSERT INTO public."TestsData" ("TestId", "Questions", "Options", "Answers")
VALUES (440091312101011, ARRAY['"[8 marks] Explain Number sets and laws of indices. Describe its meaning or purpose, principal features or steps, and one relevant example."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] Explain HCF and LCM of integers. Describe its meaning or purpose, principal features or steps, and one relevant example."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] Explain HCF and LCM of fractions and decimals. Describe its meaning or purpose, principal features or steps, and one relevant example."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] Explain Polynomial definitions and arithmetic operations. Describe its meaning or purpose, principal features or steps, and one relevant example."'::jsonb]::jsonb[], ARRAY[]::jsonb[], ARRAY['"Model answer:\nNumber sets include natural numbers, whole numbers, integers, rational numbers, irrational numbers and real numbers, with each set having specific membership rules. For a non-zero base a, powers follow laws such as a^m × a^n = a^(m+n), a^m ÷ a^n = a^(m−n), (a^m)^n = a^(mn), and a^0 = 1. These laws require attention to conditions such as non-zero denominators and the base not being zero for a^0. For example, 2^3 × 2^4 ÷ 2^5 = 2^(3+4−5) = 2^2 = 4.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should define the concept and organize the main points clearly."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nThe HCF is the greatest positive integer that divides every given integer; the LCM is the smallest positive common multiple. Prime factorization is a reliable method: 12 = 2^2 × 3 and 18 = 2 × 3^2. For HCF, take common prime factors with the smallest exponents, giving 2 × 3 = 6. For LCM, take each prime factor with the greatest exponent, giving 2^2 × 3^2 = 36. For positive integers, HCF × LCM equals the product of the two integers.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should define the concept and organize the main points clearly."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nFirst express fractions in their lowest terms. For positive fractions, the usual course rules are HCF = HCF of numerators divided by LCM of denominators, and LCM = LCM of numerators divided by HCF of denominators. Thus for 2/3 and 4/5, HCF = 2/15 and LCM = 4. For decimals, convert values to integers by multiplying by an appropriate power of ten, find the HCF/LCM, then rescale the result. Keep the decimal-place adjustment consistent and verify the resulting multiples or divisors.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should define the concept and organize the main points clearly."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nA polynomial is an expression formed from constants and variables with non-negative integer exponents and coefficients. Like terms have the same variable part and exponents, so only their coefficients are combined. For example, (2x + 3) + (x − 5) = 3x − 2. The degree of a non-zero polynomial in one variable is the highest exponent with a non-zero coefficient; the degree of 3x^4 − 2x^2 + 7 is four. Keep signs and exponents unchanged while grouping terms, and simplify before identifying the degree.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should define the concept and organize the main points clearly."'::jsonb]::jsonb[]);

-- Mathematics-I | Mid 1 | easy | TestId 440091312101012 | theory
INSERT INTO public."TestsData" ("TestId", "Questions", "Options", "Answers")
VALUES (440091312101012, ARRAY['"[8 marks] Explain Number sets and laws of indices. Describe its meaning or purpose, principal features or steps, and one relevant example."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] Explain HCF and LCM of integers. Describe its meaning or purpose, principal features or steps, and one relevant example."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] Explain Algebraic identities and their use. Describe its meaning or purpose, principal features or steps, and one relevant example."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] Explain Perimeter and area of common two-dimensional figures. Describe its meaning or purpose, principal features or steps, and one relevant example."'::jsonb]::jsonb[], ARRAY[]::jsonb[], ARRAY['"Model answer:\nNumber sets include natural numbers, whole numbers, integers, rational numbers, irrational numbers and real numbers, with each set having specific membership rules. For a non-zero base a, powers follow laws such as a^m × a^n = a^(m+n), a^m ÷ a^n = a^(m−n), (a^m)^n = a^(mn), and a^0 = 1. These laws require attention to conditions such as non-zero denominators and the base not being zero for a^0. For example, 2^3 × 2^4 ÷ 2^5 = 2^(3+4−5) = 2^2 = 4.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should define the concept and organize the main points clearly."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nThe HCF is the greatest positive integer that divides every given integer; the LCM is the smallest positive common multiple. Prime factorization is a reliable method: 12 = 2^2 × 3 and 18 = 2 × 3^2. For HCF, take common prime factors with the smallest exponents, giving 2 × 3 = 6. For LCM, take each prime factor with the greatest exponent, giving 2^2 × 3^2 = 36. For positive integers, HCF × LCM equals the product of the two integers.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should define the concept and organize the main points clearly."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nAn algebraic identity is an equality that holds for every permitted value of its variables. Common identities include (a+b)^2 = a^2 + 2ab + b^2, (a−b)^2 = a^2 − 2ab + b^2, and (a+b)(a−b) = a^2 − b^2. They support expansion, factorization and simplification. For example, (x+3)^2 = x^2 + 6x + 9. The middle term in a squared binomial is important; omitting it or using the wrong sign is a common error. Substitution can check a result.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should define the concept and organize the main points clearly."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nPerimeter measures the total distance around a plane figure; area measures the enclosed surface. For a rectangle, perimeter is 2(l+w) and area is lw. For a square, perimeter is 4a and area is a². For a triangle, area is ½ × base × perpendicular height; for a circle, circumference is 2πr and area is πr². Use compatible units, distinguish linear units from square units, and identify the required dimension rather than assuming a sloping side is the perpendicular height. State the formula, substitute values and report units.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should define the concept and organize the main points clearly."'::jsonb]::jsonb[]);

