-- Mathematics-I | ESE | easy | TestId 4400913121010125 | theory
INSERT INTO public."TestsData" ("TestId", "Questions", "Options", "Answers")
VALUES (4400913121010125, ARRAY['"[8 marks] Explain Number sets and laws of indices. Describe its meaning or purpose, principal features or steps, and one relevant example."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] Explain HCF and LCM of integers, fractions and decimals. Describe its meaning or purpose, principal features or steps, and one relevant example."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] Explain Polynomial arithmetic and algebraic identities. Describe its meaning or purpose, principal features or steps, and one relevant example."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] Explain Area and perimeter of 2D shapes. Describe its meaning or purpose, principal features or steps, and one relevant example."'::jsonb]::jsonb[], ARRAY[]::jsonb[], ARRAY['"Model answer:\nClassify numbers carefully: integers include negative whole-number values, rational numbers can be expressed as a ratio of integers with non-zero denominator, irrational values cannot be so expressed, and real numbers include rational and irrational values. For a non-zero base a, use a^m a^n = a^(m+n), a^m/a^n = a^(m−n), (a^m)^n = a^(mn), and a^0 = 1. State domain restrictions and simplify stepwise; exponents add when multiplying like bases and subtract when dividing them.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should define the concept and organize the main points clearly."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nFor integers, prime-factorize and use minimum exponents for HCF and maximum exponents for LCM. For positive fractions in lowest terms, use HCF(numerators)/LCM(denominators) for HCF, and LCM(numerators)/HCF(denominators) for LCM. For decimals, scale all values by the same power of ten to make integers, calculate HCF or LCM, then reverse the scale. Example: HCF(12,18)=6 and LCM(12,18)=36. Check that the HCF divides the original integers and the LCM is a common multiple.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should define the concept and organize the main points clearly."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nCombine only like terms, preserve signs, and use the highest non-zero exponent to determine degree. Common identities include (a+b)^2=a²+2ab+b², (a−b)^2=a²−2ab+b² and (a+b)(a−b)=a²−b². For example, (x+3)^2=x²+6x+9; the 6x middle term comes from both cross-products. Use identities to expand or factor efficiently, and verify by substitution of a convenient value when checking a potentially incorrect expression.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should define the concept and organize the main points clearly."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nUse a formula matched to the geometry and label the dimensions. Rectangle: A=lw, P=2(l+w); square: A=a², P=4a; triangle: A=½bh; parallelogram: A=bh; trapezium: A=½(a+b)h for parallel sides a and b; rhombus: A=½d₁d₂; circle: A=πr² and circumference C=2πr. Perimeter uses linear units and area uses square units. Use perpendicular height where required and do not substitute a sloping side for height.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should define the concept and organize the main points clearly."'::jsonb]::jsonb[]);

-- Mathematics-I | ESE | easy | TestId 4400913121010126 | theory
INSERT INTO public."TestsData" ("TestId", "Questions", "Options", "Answers")
VALUES (4400913121010126, ARRAY['"[8 marks] Explain Number sets and laws of indices. Describe its meaning or purpose, principal features or steps, and one relevant example."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] Explain HCF and LCM of integers, fractions and decimals. Describe its meaning or purpose, principal features or steps, and one relevant example."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] Explain Surface area and volume of solid figures. Describe its meaning or purpose, principal features or steps, and one relevant example."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] Explain Right-triangle trigonometry, angles and multiple-angle ratios. Describe its meaning or purpose, principal features or steps, and one relevant example."'::jsonb]::jsonb[], ARRAY[]::jsonb[], ARRAY['"Model answer:\nClassify numbers carefully: integers include negative whole-number values, rational numbers can be expressed as a ratio of integers with non-zero denominator, irrational values cannot be so expressed, and real numbers include rational and irrational values. For a non-zero base a, use a^m a^n = a^(m+n), a^m/a^n = a^(m−n), (a^m)^n = a^(mn), and a^0 = 1. State domain restrictions and simplify stepwise; exponents add when multiplying like bases and subtract when dividing them.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should define the concept and organize the main points clearly."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nFor integers, prime-factorize and use minimum exponents for HCF and maximum exponents for LCM. For positive fractions in lowest terms, use HCF(numerators)/LCM(denominators) for HCF, and LCM(numerators)/HCF(denominators) for LCM. For decimals, scale all values by the same power of ten to make integers, calculate HCF or LCM, then reverse the scale. Example: HCF(12,18)=6 and LCM(12,18)=36. Check that the HCF divides the original integers and the LCM is a common multiple.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should define the concept and organize the main points clearly."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nVolume measures the three-dimensional space occupied; surface area measures the area of the external surfaces. Cube: V=a³ and total surface area 6a²; cuboid: V=lwh and TSA=2(lw+lh+wh); cylinder: V=πr²h and TSA=2πr(h+r); cone: V=⅓πr²h and total surface area πr(l+r), where l is slant height; sphere: V=4/3πr³ and area 4πr². Match the formula to the solid, use radius rather than diameter, keep units consistent and distinguish curved from total surface area.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should define the concept and organize the main points clearly."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nFor an acute angle in a right triangle, sinθ=opposite/hypotenuse, cosθ=adjacent/hypotenuse and tanθ=opposite/adjacent. Select the ratio matching the known and unknown sides. If a vertical height h is observed from horizontal distance d at angle θ, tanθ=h/d, so h=d tanθ, subject to the stated geometry. Use the correct angle mode and units. Unit-circle definitions extend trigonometric ratios beyond acute angles; identities such as sin2θ=2sinθcosθ help with multiple-angle questions. Check that the result is plausible for the given angle.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should define the concept and organize the main points clearly."'::jsonb]::jsonb[]);

